# The Story

```markdown
**Title:** Normalize free-text species names to canonical species codes before saving a sighting

**Type:** Feature
**Size:** S
**Epic/Component:** Ingest Pipeline — Species Normalization
**Priority:** Medium *(not specified by requester; defaulting and stating the assumption — data-quality risk of unnormalized species text reaching stored records, but no urgency signal was given)*
**Depends On:** None

---

## Effort Estimate

**Recommended Model Tier:** Standard
**Reasoning:** A single, well-bounded pure function with clear input/output semantics and no architectural decisions needed is squarely Standard-tier work — Economy risks missing edge-case normalization logic (aliases, casing, whitespace, unmatched input), and Premium is unwarranted since there is no design tradeoff to make.

---

## User Story

**As a** Fernglen product owner
**I want to** normalize free-text species names entered by field volunteers onto the app's canonical species-code list before a sighting record is saved
**So that** every persisted sighting record carries a consistent, queryable canonical species code instead of raw, volunteer-entered free text

---

## Acceptance Criteria

- [ ] `normalize_species_code(raw_name: str) -> str` returns the canonical species code for free-text input that matches a known name or alias in the canonical species-code list (case-insensitive, leading/trailing whitespace tolerant).
- [ ] `normalize_species_code()` returns a defined, documented not-found result (e.g., `None`, or a specific documented exception) for free-text input with no match in the canonical species-code list — an unmatched input never silently passes through as an invalid code.
- [ ] The ingest pipeline calls `normalize_species_code()` on the volunteer-entered species text before a sighting record is saved, so every persisted record carries a canonical species code (or the defined not-found handling), never raw free text.
- [ ] `normalize_species_code()` is pure: given the same input and the same canonical species-code list, it returns the same output every time, with no I/O and no mutation of shared state.

---

## Technical Notes

**Dependencies:**
- An existing canonical species-code list/lookup accessible to the ingest pipeline (assumed to already exist per the request's wording — "the app's canonical species-code list" — but not independently verifiable in this working copy; see Comments).

**Constraints:**
- None specified by the requester.

**Files to Create/Modify:**
- `ingest/normalize.py` — add `normalize_species_code()`. Note: this path does not currently exist in the working repository checked for this story (the checkout contains no application code at all); confirm whether the file already exists in the live Fernglen repo or needs to be created fresh.
- The ingest pipeline's save-sighting call site — wire in the `normalize_species_code()` call ahead of persistence. Exact call site not identified (no ingest code present in this checkout).

**Architecture Notes:**
- Pure function: no network/disk I/O, deterministic mapping only.
- Matching strategy (exact match, case-insensitive, alias table, fuzzy matching) is left to implementation — Negotiable per INVEST — but the not-found behavior must be explicit and documented, not implicit.

